  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthening and extending social protection coverage</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Government of South Africa is reforming its social security system. One of the most notable gaps in the social security system is the absence of a public fund that provides pensions and life insurance to the workforce. Many private occupational and voluntary schemes have been established to fill this gap, but some 6.2 million formal sector workers &#xe2;&#x20ac;&#x201d; including low-income earners - are excluded from such arrangements. The principal reform proposal is therefore the establishment of a National Social Security Fund (NSSF). The country also faces a gap in the availability, quality and use of social protection data, with such information often spread across different systems and institutions.  Without reliable, accessible and up-to-date statistics, it becomes hard to design effective social protection policies. South Africa ratified Convention 190 on Violence and Harassment, and 155 on Occupational Safety and Health (OSH) and has an obligation to prevent violence and harassment at work, and to promote workplace health and safety.    In collaboration with research and academic institutions, international experts and with funding support by the Government of South Africa, ongoing institutional and individual capacity building support is provided for public officers to drive the social security reform agenda. Technical and capacity support is directed towards the review of the Green Paper on the Comprehensive Social Security and Retirement Reform. To this end technical support focuses on refining the design of the social security reform model, including conducting actuarial valuations of options for a sustainable National Social Security Fund. Technical and capacity-building support is also directed towards establishing a community of practice on national social protection statistics in South Africa.  Technical assistance is being provided to a tripartite Ergonomics Technical Committee to draft a Code of Practice for the Elimination of Violence and Harassment through OS</narrative>
  </description>
